from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':('Tourism accommodation, guiding and passenger services are tertiary activities.','Tourism demand can support primary suppliers and secondary manufacturers without changing their underlying activity classification.'),
'02':('Leisure concerns time beyond obligations and chosen activities, while tourism concerns temporary visitor trips beyond the usual environment.','Tourism can have leisure, business or other relevant visitor purposes, and leisure can occur without a tourism trip.'),
'02-01':('Local recreation can be leisure without being tourism.','The appearance of relaxation or spending does not by itself establish visitor status.'),
'02-02':('Tourism includes domestic and international visitor activity beyond the usual environment.','Visits, unique visitors, person-nights and room-nights are different counting units.'),
'03':('Namibian visitor activities include nature, wildlife, coastal, heritage and cultural experiences.','Activities require appropriate access, services and management for their particular resource and setting.'),
'04':('Domestic tourism concerns residents travelling within their country beyond the usual environment.','Inbound and outbound international tourism describe the receiving and originating country perspectives on cross-country visitor trips.'),
'04-01':('A Namibian resident’s holiday visit to Etosha is domestic tourism within Namibia.','Residence rather than passport nationality is needed for domestic visitor classification.'),
'04-02':('A non-resident visiting Namibia is an inbound international visitor from Namibia’s perspective.','A resident travelling abroad is outbound from the home country and inbound to the destination.'),
'05':('Tourism can link spending to jobs, suppliers, infrastructure, facilities and cultural exchange under suitable arrangements.','Arrivals or revenue alone do not establish equal household benefits or profitability.'),
'05-01':('Visitor receipts can pay workers and suppliers, while some spending may leave the assessed area.','Inbound international spending can contribute foreign exchange, with net benefit affected by outward payments.'),
'05-02':('Tourism supports direct jobs and potentially indirect supplier work.','Employment headcount needs duration, hours and access evidence for a meaningful livelihood comparison.'),
'05-03':('Tourism-related infrastructure can benefit residents where shared access, capacity and affordability permit.','An installed road or utility connection does not by itself establish every household’s access.'),
'05-04':('Visitor facilities enable activities and stays but need demand, maintenance and usable access.','Facility construction is different from demonstrated service use or community benefit.'),
'05-05':('Respectful visitor exchange can support chosen cultural presentation, crafts and learning.','Cultural benefit depends on host control and consent rather than contact alone.'),
'06':('Tourism can impose seasonal, environmental, resource, social and price pressures as well as benefits.','Costs and benefits can differ between stakeholders, places and periods.'),
'06-01':('Seasonal demand can reduce labour needs or facility use during quiet periods.','Room occupancy uses occupied room-nights divided by available room-nights, not visitor totals.'),
'06-02':('Tourism traffic can contribute congestion where movement exceeds usable capacity.','Vehicle counts alone do not establish delays, tourist status or the whole destination’s traffic change.'),
'06-03':('Tourism waste and emissions can affect receiving environments where management is inadequate.','A litter observation establishes a condition but not the identity of every source or annual total.'),
'06-04':('Tourism demand can contribute a water gap where combined demand exceeds usable supply.','Lower use per guest can coexist with higher total demand when guest numbers grow sufficiently.'),
'06-05':('Trampling and unsuitable vehicle or building use can affect vegetation, surfaces and runoff.','Footpath width can reflect construction, substrate, rain and maintenance as well as visitor use.'),
'06-06':('Tourism can create privacy, access, representation and benefit-sharing conflicts.','Visitor satisfaction alone does not establish resident consent or equal experience.'),
'06-07':('External purchases and payments reduce retention within the specified local or national boundary.','Feasible local sourcing depends on suitable quantity, quality, timing and service capacity.'),
'06-08':('Visitor demand can contribute price pressure where supply responds slowly.','Observed price rises require evidence about demand and other costs before attributing causation to tourism.'),
'07':('Transport, information, capital and organisation interact in tourism development.','An intervention must address the actual constraint and be assessed through outcomes matching its aim.'),
'07-01':('Access improvements can reduce particular journey constraints but may also increase pressure at a sensitive site.','Publicity does not repair a closed route or provide a safe feasible alternative.'),
'07-02':('Accurate marketing can improve awareness and enquiries where suitable services exist.','Enquiries, bookings and completed visits are distinct outcomes.'),
'07-03':('Tourism capital needs include initial assets and continuing operation or maintenance.','A viable funding proposal needs demand, cost and risk evidence rather than a need for money alone.'),
'07-04':('Effective organisation connects responsibilities, training, records and maintenance to reliable services.','An agreement or training attendance record does not establish fulfilment or improved outcomes by itself.'),
'91':('Environmental-quality assessment requires defined indicators, consistent scoring direction and comparable observations.','A falling total does not prove that every component indicator declined.'),
'92':('Footpath investigation requires defined edges, consistent repeated measurements, matched use counts and relevant contextual records.','A relationship between use and width or depth does not alone isolate visitor-caused erosion.'),
'93':('Questionnaire interpretation requires a defined target population, neutral wording, clear denominators and recorded selection or non-response.','Seasonal perceptions and recalled responses differ from direct matched observations across seasons.')}
CARDS={
'001':('Name the sector containing guiding and holiday accommodation services.','Tertiary industry.',['1 mark for the sector name tertiary.']),
'002':('Describe the activity pattern: A grows food for a lodge, B manufactures its furniture and C guides its guests.','A is primary production, B secondary manufacturing and C a tertiary service. Tourism demand links them but does not give all three the same activity classification.',['1 mark for each correct activity–sector relationship using the supplied evidence.']),
'003':('Name the term for visitors’ temporary trips beyond their usual environment, distinct from moving to take resident employment.','Tourism.',['1 mark for the term name tourism.']),
'004':('Describe the contrast: A plays football near home during free time; B takes a holiday beyond the usual environment; C makes a temporary conference visit elsewhere.','A illustrates leisure without a necessary tourism trip; B combines leisure and tourism; C can be tourism with a business rather than leisure purpose.',['1 mark for each distinct activity/purpose relationship supported by the evidence.']),
'005':('Name the term for free time beyond obligations and chosen activities such as local recreation.','Leisure.',['1 mark for the term name leisure.']),
'006':('Name the visitor-count unit generated by two people staying three nights.','Person-nights: six person-nights.',['1 mark for the unit name person-nights.']),
'007':('Name one nature-based tourist activity associated with Etosha.','Wildlife viewing or an appropriately managed guided nature/game-viewing visit.',['1 mark for a relevant activity name.']),
'008':('Describe the service-needs pattern: walkers use a maintained path, overnight guests need accommodation/water services and guided wildlife visitors need appropriate guiding/access.','Different activities create different service requirements. Paths support walking, accommodation services support stays and guiding/access support wildlife visits. The activity evidence does not establish one identical footprint for all tourism.',['1 mark for the activity/service relationship; 1 for a distinct supplied evidence example; 1 for the differentiated-impact interpretation.']),
'009':('Distinguish domestic from international tourism using a Namibian resident’s holiday within Namibia and a non-resident’s holiday in Namibia.','The resident’s within-country visit is domestic; the non-resident’s visit is inbound international from Namibia’s perspective. Residence and the country of reference distinguish the categories, not journey length alone.',['1 mark for the domestic side of the difference; 1 for the contrasting inbound international case; 1 for the residence/reference-country basis.']),
'010':('Suggest the tourism classification from Namibia’s perspective for a Namibian resident visiting a destination abroad, explaining the reference-country issue.','Outbound international tourism from Namibia. The trip leaves the country of residence; it is inbound from the destination country’s perspective.',['1 mark for the classification in the given context; 1 for the residence/border reason; 1 for the alternative-perspective application.']),
'011':('Name the tourism category of a Namibian resident’s holiday visit to Etosha.','Domestic tourism within Namibia.',['1 mark for the category name domestic.']),
'012':('Name the international category, from Namibia’s perspective, of a non-resident’s temporary holiday in Namibia.','Inbound international tourism.',['1 mark for the category name inbound international.']),
'013':('Explain how suitable local purchasing by a lodge can spread part of visitor spending within the area.','Purchases create demand for local suppliers. Suppliers receive receipts that can support wages and production, and some recipients may spend locally again. The effect depends on actual local sourcing and usable supply rather than arrivals alone.',['1 mark for local purchases as a factor; 1 for the supplier-demand link; 1 for the wage/production flow; 1 for a conditional further local-income effect.']),
'014':('Describe the supplied tourism spending chart: receipts N$20,000, payments N$8,000 to local workers/suppliers and N$7,000 outside the area, with N$5,000 retained before other unspecified obligations.','The chart separates local payments, outward flows and a retained balance. Local payments are N$8,000 and outward payments N$7,000. The N$5,000 balance is not established as final profit or equal household benefit.',['1 mark for the flow pattern; 1 for paired chart-value evidence; 1 for the bounded retained-balance interpretation.']),
'015':('Name the external-earnings benefit that inbound international visitor spending can contribute.','Foreign exchange earnings.',['1 mark for the benefit name foreign exchange earnings.']),
'016':('Name one direct tourism job rather than a manufacturing supplier job.','A guide, accommodation receptionist or other appropriate direct visitor-service role.',['1 mark for a relevant direct-job name.']),
'017':('Name an infrastructure improvement that can serve visitors and residents where shared access is available.','A usable access road, communications link or suitable shared utility system.',['1 mark for a relevant infrastructure name.']),
'018':('Name one visitor facility, distinct from the natural attraction itself.','Accommodation, toilets, a visitor centre or another relevant visitor facility.',['1 mark for a relevant facility name.']),
'019':('Name one cultural benefit possible where hosts choose and control respectful visitor presentation.','Support for chosen craft work, heritage interpretation or mutual cultural learning.',['1 mark for a relevant cultural-benefit point.']),
'020':('Explain how a peak in visitor water demand can contribute to shortages where total supply is fixed and other users already need most of it.','Additional accommodation and service use raises total demand. If combined requirements exceed usable supply, a gap develops. Without a suitable response, rationing or reduced access can affect other users, depending on allocation.',['1 mark for visitor demand as a factor; 1 for the combined-demand link; 1 for the supply-gap mechanism; 1 for the conditional access effect.']),
'021':('Describe the demand record: usable supply is 100 m³/day, residents need 70 and tourism needs 40; no additional supply or storage release is available.','Combined need is 110 m³/day, ten above supply. The resource supports a water-balance gap at the stated time. It does not establish how the shortage is allocated between users.',['1 mark for the combined-demand relationship; 1 for the ten-m³/day evidence; 1 for the allocation limitation.']),
'022':('Name the employment problem when quiet-season demand leads enterprises to end temporary contracts.','Seasonal unemployment.',['1 mark for the problem name seasonal unemployment.']),
'023':('Name the transport condition indicated by queues and journey delays where arrival demand exceeds usable road capacity.','Congestion.',['1 mark for the condition name congestion.']),
'024':('Name one pollution pressure requiring tourism waste management.','Litter, unsuitable wastewater discharge, excessive noise or another appropriately stated pressure.',['1 mark for a relevant pollution-pressure name.']),
'025':('Name the resource shortage when combined daily requirements exceed usable water supply.','A water-supply gap or water shortage.',['1 mark for the shortage name.']),
'026':('Name one pathway of landscape damage from repeated unmanaged walking on vulnerable ground.','Trampling removes vegetation and can expose soil to erosion.',['1 mark for a relevant damage-pathway point.']),
'027':('Name one socio-cultural cost that can occur where visitors disregard residents’ wishes.','Loss of privacy, disrespectful representation or unwanted interference with chosen practices.',['1 mark for a relevant socio-cultural cost name.']),
'028':('Name the spending flow when a tourism enterprise pays suppliers outside the local area being assessed.','Leakage from that local economy.',['1 mark for the flow name leakage.']),
'029':('Name the possible price pressure where visitor demand rises while relevant short-term supply is limited.','Higher local prices or price inflation for the affected goods/services.',['1 mark for a relevant price-effect point.']),
'030':('Explain why poor booking organisation can prevent improved publicity from increasing completed visits.','Publicity can create enquiries, but booking failures interrupt conversion into reservations and arrivals. Potential customers may not complete the trip, leaving visits or receipts unchanged. The response needs reliable booking alongside accurate information.',['1 mark for publicity as the demand factor; 1 for the enquiry-to-booking link; 1 for the organisational interruption; 1 for its completed-visit effect.']),
'031':('Describe the record: enquiries rise from 100 to 200 after improved information, but completed visits remain 40 and booking failures are reported.','Interest and realised visits show different trends. Enquiries double while completed visits do not rise. The evidence identifies a booking constraint and does not establish that publicity alone has met the arrival objective.',['1 mark for the differing pattern; 1 for paired value evidence; 1 for the bounded objective interpretation.']),
'032':('How far do you agree that more advertising solves this campsite’s access problem? Its only feasible approach is closed by a damaged bridge, with no safe alternative arranged. Judge usable visitor access.','Disagree. Advertising can communicate an offer but does not restore a usable route. The supplied physical access barrier remains; repair or a safe feasible alternative is needed before publicity can deliver accessible visits. More enquiries would not demonstrate repaired access.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using route-closure evidence, the publicity/access mechanism distinction and a relevant conditional remedy.','1 C mark for a supported decision against the access claim.']),
'033':('How far do you agree that 200 enquiries prove a marketing project achieved its target of 80 completed visits? The record shows only 40 completed visits and no missing arrival records.','Disagree. Enquiries are interest, while the target concerns completed visits. The 40 arrivals are 40 below the target. Marketing may have improved awareness, but the stated arrival goal is not met; booking and access conditions need examination.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using evidence about the different units, 40-visit gap and relevant conversion constraints.','1 C mark for a supported decision against achieved target.']),
'034':('How far do you agree that funding construction alone makes a visitor centre viable? The plan has no provision for staff, maintenance or evidence of sufficient demand.','Disagree with viability being established. Construction provides an asset but not continuing service delivery or revenue. Staff and maintenance needs remain, and absent demand evidence leaves utilisation uncertain. A feasible operating and funding plan is needed.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the capital/operation distinction and missing staffing, maintenance and demand evidence.','1 C mark for a supported viability decision.']),
'035':('How far do you agree that a signed tourism joint-venture agreement proves fair benefits have reached community members? No payment, distribution or participation records are supplied.','Disagree that receipt and fairness are established. The agreement sets arrangements, but fulfilment and distribution need records and affected members’ evidence. Potential benefits may exist without demonstrated delivery; monitoring and understandable reporting are relevant.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the agreement/outcome distinction, missing records and participation evidence.','1 C mark for a supported decision about demonstrated benefit delivery.']),
'036':('Describe a sequence for recording the supplied environmental-quality assessment sheet, which uses three indicators scored 0 poor, 1 intermediate and 2 good.','Check the category anchors and scoring direction; observe each indicator consistently for the agreed period; record component scores and sum them out of six, retaining notes.',['1 mark for the first scale/anchor step; 1 for consistent observation; 1 for the recorded components/total result.']),
'037':('Explain why observers should practise using the same environmental-quality scoring anchors before comparing sites.','Different personal interpretations can give different scores for the same condition. Shared practice and clearer anchors reduce that inconsistency, improving comparability without removing all judgement.',['1 mark for the subjectivity reason; 1 for the comparability improvement because shared anchors reduce disagreement.']),
'038':('Describe a sequence for measuring footpath width at the stations on the supplied investigation plan.','Define edges and tape orientation; measure perpendicular widths consistently with repeats at each chosen station; record readings and units, then calculate the mean for each station.',['1 mark for the first definition/orientation step; 1 for the repeated measurement sequence; 1 for the unit-labelled record and mean result.']),
'039':('Explain why one ten-minute pedestrian count cannot establish that present users caused the entire measured path width.','Width reflects a longer history and can also depend on construction, slope, surface or maintenance. The short count can show current use, but the timing and alternative factors limit causal attribution.',['1 mark for the timescale/alternative-factor reason; 1 for explaining why association differs from isolated causation.']),
'040':('Describe a sequence for the supplied seasonal questionnaire sheet, recording whether residents report an income benefit during the stated period.','State the resident target group and apply the planned selection rule; ask the same neutral question with voluntary participation and record responses/non-response; summarise counts with the correct sample denominator for each period.',['1 mark for the first target/selection step; 1 for consistent voluntary questioning; 1 for the recorded response/denominator result.']),
'041':('Explain why questioning only visitors cannot establish residents’ views about tourism’s effects.','The sampled population is visitors, while the claim concerns residents. A suitably selected resident sample is needed because the groups can experience different costs and benefits.',['1 mark for the population-mismatch reason; 1 for explaining the relevant resident-evidence remedy.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','04','05'],'prompt':'(a) Name the economic sector containing guiding. [1]\n(b) Describe three distinct ways tourism can benefit a host area under suitable arrangements. [3]\n(c) Name a Namibian resident’s tourism category for a holiday within Namibia. [1]\n(d) Describe the contrast in this record: A has 20 visitors spending N$4,000; B has 60 visitors spending N$6,000 over the same recorded interval and categories. [2]','answer':'(a) Tertiary. (b) Appropriate examples include employment income, local supplier demand, usable shared infrastructure, facilities or host-controlled cultural exchange. (c) Domestic. (d) B has greater total recorded spending, but the mean is N$100 per visitor compared with A’s N$200. These are receipts/spending measures, not profit.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for tertiary.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct benefit point descriptions linked to host use or income.','(c) 1 A mark for domestic.','(d) 2 B marks for the total comparison and the correct paired mean-spending interpretation.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['05','06','06-01'],'prompt':'A hypothetical lodge has 20 rooms available for ten nights. It sells 120 room-nights. It receives N$20,000 and pays N$8,000 to local workers/suppliers and N$7,000 outside the local area. Other obligations and final profit are unspecified.\n(a) Name the outward spending flow from the assessed local economy. [1]\n(b) Calculate room occupancy. [2]\n(c) Describe three distinct capacity or spending features supported by the resource. [3]\n(d) What judgement is supported about the claim “the remaining N$5,000 proves equal household benefit”? [1]','answer':'(a) Leakage. (b) Available capacity is 20×10 = 200 room-nights; the occupied fraction is 120/200 = 0.6, equivalent to 60% occupancy. (c) Eighty room-nights are unsold; N$8,000 supports stated local payments; N$7,000 leaves the local boundary; N$5,000 remains before unspecified obligations. Credit three distinct supported features. (d) Reject the claim: a balance does not identify its distribution or establish equal household benefit.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for leakage.','(b) 2 B marks for the matched room-night method and 60% result.','(c) 3 B marks for three different patterns supported by resource evidence, without double-crediting occupancy and its equivalent unused share.','(d) 1 C mark for the supported distribution judgement.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Calculate',2,'B'),('c','Describe',3,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['05','07','07-04'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that higher tourism investment and employment prove every conservancy household gained income? The June 2017 MCC evaluation of Namibia’s Conservancy Support Activity reported investment and employment gains, but its analysis generally did not establish major household income or expenditure increases. Judge what those dated findings support. [5]','answer':'Disagree with the universal household claim. Investment and employment gains are relevant benefits, but they measure different outcomes from household income distribution. The evaluation’s household findings limit that inference. Benefits can be uneven and agreements need monitoring. The dated programme evidence cannot describe every conservancy today.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the enterprise/household distinction, the evaluation’s bounded evidence and distribution or time limits.','1 C mark for a supported overall decision that recognises gains without asserting universal household benefit.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]},
{'objectives':['05','06','91'],'prompt':'A teaching investigation assesses three sites during matched ten-minute periods. Higher quality scores mean better conditions: 0 poor, 1 intermediate, 2 good for each of litter, noise and facilities. Resource table: S1 visitors 10, scores 2/2/2; S2 visitors 30, scores 1/1/2; S3 visitors 50, scores 0/0/2. H1: total assessed quality declines as visitor counts rise. H2: facilities scores decline as visitor counts rise. Use the supplied scoring sheet and plotting axes; a horizontal axis shows visitors from 0 to 60 and a vertical axis total quality from 0 to 6.\n(a) Name the economic sector of guiding. [1]\n(b) Describe three possible tourism effects relevant to an environmental assessment. [3]\n(c) Name two tourism services that can add water demand. [2]\n(d) Calculate the mean total quality score across the three sites. [3]\n(e) Describe the relationship and a component exception using the table. [3]\n(f) What conclusion about H1 is supported, including its evidence limit? [3]\n(g) What conclusion about H2 is supported? [3]\n(h) Design a fair test of the two stated hypotheses. [3]\n(i) Describe a usable site-selection and scoring procedure. [3]\n(j) Complete the total-quality entry for S2 on the supplied sheet. [2]\n(k) Plot S1 and S3 on the provided axes. [2]\n(l) Explain one specific limitation and a corresponding improvement. [2]','answer':'(a) Tertiary. (b) Relevant descriptions include litter/waste pressure, noise disturbance, vegetation damage or pressure on facilities/resources. (c) Accommodation laundry and guest washing, or two distinct suitable services. (d) Totals are 6,4,2; mean (6+4+2)/3 = 4 out of 6. (e) Quality falls as counts rise; litter and noise scores fall, but facilities remain 2. (f) H1 is supported at these sampled sites: 6 at 10 visitors falls to 2 at 50; association does not isolate visitor causation. (g) H2 is unsupported: facilities remain 2 at all three counts. (h) Retain the two measurable hypotheses, use comparable sites and matched periods, and record counts with defined component scores. (i) Use a defined selection frame and random, systematic or stratified rule as appropriate; apply the same scoring anchors, record components and context. (j) S2 total 1+1+2 = 4 out of 6. (k) Coordinates (10,6) and (50,2). (l) Subjective anchors can differ between observers; shared practice and clearer descriptors improve comparability. Other specific matched limitation/improvement pairs are valid.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for tertiary.','(b) 3 A marks for three different effect point descriptions.','(c) 2 A marks for two distinct service names.','(d) 3 B marks for deriving totals/mean method, answer 4 and the out-of-six scale.','(e) 3 B marks for the inverse data relationship, a component pattern and the facilities exception.','(f) 3 C marks for the H1 conclusion, paired evidence and the causal/coverage limit.','(g) 3 C marks for the H2 conclusion, constant-score evidence and the total/component distinction.','(h) 3 D marks for usable hypothesis testing: matched sites/periods, count method and operational scoring.','(i) 3 D marks for selection rule, consistent scoring and a usable recording sequence.','(j) 2 D marks for correct component use and the recorded total 4.','(k) 2 D marks, one per correctly plotted coordinate on supplied scales.','(l) 2 D marks for a specific evaluation limitation and an improvement addressing it.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-2.3-01 scoring-sheet and supplied-axes variant for P04']},
{'objectives':['06','06-05','92'],'prompt':'A teaching footpath investigation uses matched ten-minute pedestrian counts. At A, count 20 and widths 100,110,120 cm; at B, count 40 and widths 140,150,160 cm; at C, count 60 and widths 120,130,140 cm. Measured incision depths are A 1 cm, B 2 cm, C 3 cm. H1: mean path width increases at every step as counts rise. H2: measured depth increases as counts rise. The plan marks stations A/B/C, perpendicular width lines and a reference for depth. Supplied plotting axes show visitors 0–80 horizontally and mean width 0–200 cm vertically.\n(a) Name the loss-and-movement process that can deepen an exposed path. [1]\n(b) Describe three links from repeated unmanaged foot traffic to landscape damage. [3]\n(c) Name two non-visitor influences on path form. [2]\n(d) Calculate mean width at B, including units. [3]\n(e) Describe the width and depth patterns, using an exception. [3]\n(f) What conclusion about H1 is supported? [3]\n(g) What conclusion about H2 and causation is supported? [3]\n(h) Design a test using the two stated hypotheses. [3]\n(i) Describe a consistent measurement and recording sequence. [3]\n(j) Complete the mean-width entries for A and C on the supplied sheet. [2]\n(k) Plot A and C on the provided axes. [2]\n(l) Explain a limitation of the short counts and a specific improvement. [2]','answer':'(a) Erosion. (b) Trampling can remove cover; exposed/compacted ground can concentrate runoff under suitable conditions; flowing water can remove soil and change path form. (c) Slope, surface material, rainfall, construction or maintenance: any two distinct factors. (d) (140+150+160)/3 = 150 cm. (e) Widths are 110,150,130 cm: C is narrower than B despite higher count; depths rise 1,2,3 cm. (f) H1 is not fully supported: A to B rises, B to C falls, contradicting the every-step prediction. (g) H2 is supported by the depth series, but the short use record and other factors prevent isolated attribution of accumulated erosion to current users. (h) Use comparable safe sites, matched counts and defined width/depth readings to test both specified relationships. (i) Define edges/orientation, repeat readings consistently and record raw values, units and context before means. (j) A 110 cm; C 130 cm. (k) (20,110) and (60,130). (l) A ten-minute count may not represent historical or seasonal use; repeated matched counts across relevant times improve coverage but still require contextual evidence.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for erosion.','(b) 3 A marks for three coherent landscape-effect point descriptions.','(c) 2 A marks for two distinct relevant factor names.','(d) 3 B marks for mean method, answer 150 and cm unit.','(e) 3 B marks for the non-monotonic width data, its specific exception and the increasing depth pattern.','(f) 3 C marks for the qualified H1 conclusion, supporting A/B evidence and contradictory B/C evidence.','(g) 3 C marks for the H2 conclusion, depth evidence and the causal/timescale limitation.','(h) 3 D marks for comparable selection, matched counting and measurable width/depth design linked to each hypothesis.','(i) 3 D marks for consistent edge/orientation method, repeated readings and unit-labelled records.','(j) 2 D marks, one per completed mean.','(k) 2 D marks, one per correct plotted coordinate on supplied axes.','(l) 2 D marks for the specific evaluation limitation and corresponding temporal-coverage improvement.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-2.3-02 station-plan, record-sheet and supplied-axes variant for P05']},
{'objectives':['05','06','93'],'prompt':'A teaching survey compares matched quiet and busy periods. The same eligible group of twenty residents voluntarily answers yes or no in each period to the neutral question “During this period, did tourism improve your household income?” All twenty answer both times. Yes responses: quiet 12, busy 8. Matched ten-minute visitor counts: quiet 20, busy 60. H1: the share reporting an income benefit is higher in the busy period. H2: visitor counts are higher in the busy period. The supplied form records totals/denominators; provided bar-chart axes have period categories and percentages from 0 to 100.\n(a) Name the direct benefit represented by wages from a guiding job. [1]\n(b) Describe three possible disadvantages of seasonal tourism demand. [3]\n(c) Name two different tourism-related services a visitor may use. [2]\n(d) Calculate the quiet-period positive-response percentage. [3]\n(e) Describe the response and visitor-count contrasts using values. [3]\n(f) What conclusion about H1 is supported? [3]\n(g) What conclusion about H2 and wider generalisation is supported? [3]\n(h) Design a suitable test of the two stated hypotheses. [3]\n(i) Describe an ethical selection, questioning and recording sequence. [3]\n(j) Complete the busy-period percentage and its sample denominator on the supplied form. [2]\n(k) Plot the two percentage bars on the provided axes. [2]\n(l) Explain one limitation of the sample and a corresponding improvement. [2]','answer':'(a) Employment income. (b) Reduced quiet-season jobs/hours, underused capacity with continuing costs, or household income instability: three distinct relevant descriptions. (c) Guiding, accommodation, catering or transport: two distinct services. (d) The fraction is 12/20 = 0.6, equivalent to 60%. (e) Positive share falls from 60% to 40%, while visitor counts rise from 20 to 60; the resident-response denominator stays twenty in both periods. (f) H1 is rejected for the sample: the busy share is twenty percentage points lower, not higher. (g) H2 is supported by 60 versus 20; two matched observations do not establish the full year or all resident experiences. (h) State both predictions, record neutral responses from an appropriate resident sample and repeat matched visitor counts across the compared periods. (i) Define residents and a clear random/systematic/stratified selection rule, seek voluntary participation and record responses/non-response without identifying details. (j) Forty per cent; denominator twenty residents. (k) Quiet bar 60%, busy bar 40%. (l) Twenty selected residents may omit important groups or locations; broaden appropriate stratified coverage with a clear within-group rule, while retaining neutral questions and matched periods.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for employment income.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct seasonal-cost point descriptions.','(c) 2 A marks for two service names.','(d) 3 B marks for percentage method, answer 60 and percent unit.','(e) 3 B marks for the response-share decline, visitor-count increase and stable sample denominator, each supported by data.','(f) 3 C marks for the H1 conclusion, paired share evidence and prediction-versus-result judgement.','(g) 3 C marks for the H2 conclusion, count evidence and coverage/generalisation limit.','(h) 3 D marks for hypothesis-linked resident evidence, matched count method and period design.','(i) 3 D marks for eligible selection, voluntary neutral questioning and usable anonymous recording.','(j) 2 D marks for the percentage and correct denominator.','(k) 2 D marks, one for each bar height on the supplied scale.','(l) 2 D marks for the specific sampling evaluation and a suitable coverage improvement.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-2.3-03 questionnaire, response-sheet and supplied-axes variant for P06']},
{'objectives':['06','07','91','93'],'prompt':'A teaching before-and-after investigation compares managed site M with reference site R over matched ten-minute periods. Litter pieces: M 20 before and 8 after; R 18 before and 16 after. Visitor counts at each site rise from 40 before to 80 after. A new bin/collection arrangement begins at M, but other influences are not fully controlled. H1: litter reduction is greater at M than R. H2: visitor counts fall after management begins. The supplied paired-site form has before/after columns; provided axes show before/after categories and litter pieces from 0 to 25, with a key for M/R.\n(a) Name one waste pressure relevant to tourism. [1]\n(b) Describe three functions of effective visitor-site management. [3]\n(c) Name two other influences that should be recorded when comparing litter. [2]\n(d) Calculate the percentage reduction in litter pieces at M. [3]\n(e) Describe three distinct paired patterns using the data. [3]\n(f) What conclusion about H1 and the management cause is supported? [3]\n(g) What conclusion about H2 is supported? [3]\n(h) Design a before-and-after test using the two hypotheses and a reference site. [3]\n(i) Describe a consistent count and recording procedure. [3]\n(j) Complete the absolute litter reductions for M and R on the supplied form. [2]\n(k) Plot M’s before and after litter points on the provided axes. [2]\n(l) Explain one specific limitation and a corresponding improvement. [2]','answer':'(a) Litter or a relevant named waste pressure. (b) Relevant functions include suitable waste collection, maintained paths/facilities, reliable responsibilities and monitoring. (c) Weather, general clean-up timing, other users or collection schedule: any two distinct relevant influences. (d) (20−8)/20×100 = 60%. (e) M falls twelve pieces while R falls two; both visitor counts double; M has less litter after despite more observed visitors. (f) H1 is supported by twelve versus two fewer pieces, but uncontrolled influences mean the bin arrangement alone is not established as the sole cause. (g) H2 is rejected: visitor counts rise from 40 to 80 at both sites. (h) State both predictions, retain a comparable reference and repeat matched before/after observations with relevant context. (i) Define count boundaries and pieces, use equal durations/areas, record raw totals and visitor counts before calculating changes. (j) M twelve pieces; R two pieces. (k) M before 20, after 8 on their respective categories. (l) An unmatched cleaning schedule could affect litter independently; match or record it and repeat observations across comparable periods.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for a waste-pressure name.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct management-function point descriptions.','(c) 2 A marks for two other-factor names.','(d) 3 B marks for reduction-over-original method, answer 60 and percent unit.','(e) 3 B marks for three distinct paired patterns supported by data.','(f) 3 C marks for the H1 conclusion, reference comparison and causal-attribution limit.','(g) 3 C marks for the H2 conclusion, paired count evidence and prediction/result distinction.','(h) 3 D marks for hypothesis-linked reference design, matched before/after timing and relevant control/context records.','(i) 3 D marks for operational definition, matched count method and usable records.','(j) 2 D marks, one for each absolute reduction.','(k) 2 D marks, one per correct point on the supplied axes and key.','(l) 2 D marks for the specific evaluation limitation and a matched improvement.'],'split':{'A':6,'B':6,'C':6,'D':12},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',2,'A'),('d','Calculate',3,'B'),('e','Describe',3,'B'),('f','What',3,'C'),('g','What',3,'C'),('h','Design',3,'D'),('i','Describe',3,'D'),('j','Complete',2,'D'),('k','Plot',2,'D'),('l','Explain',2,'D')],'assets_required':['VIS-6137-2.3-01 paired-count sheet variant for P07','VIS-6137-2.3-04 before/after supplied-axes variant for P07']}
]
